"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3422],{73422:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-03","slug":"base-03","trackId":"base","order":4,"title":"How to Store Cryptocurrency","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":7,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-04"],"markdown":"Lesson 3 \xb7 4 min\\n\\n## Wallets\\n\\nHow to Store Cryptocurrency Correctly\\n\\nThe previous article raises an obvious question:\\n\\nwhere is cryptocurrency actually stored, and how do you stop someone stealing it?\\n\\nFirst, cryptocurrency is not inside your phone, computer or even the small device called a cold wallet. It exists on the blockchain. A wallet stores what lets you control it: private keys. Think of the blockchain as an enormous vault containing many compartments.\\n\\nYour funds are in one compartment, and the private key lets you control them. Wallet security therefore means protecting your keys above all else.\\n\\n### Hot and Cold Wallets\\n\\nCryptocurrency wallets are generally divided into two broad categories:\\n- hot;\\n- cold.\\n\\n#### Hot Wallet\\n\\nA hot wallet runs on an internet-connected device, for example:\\n- a phone app;\\n- a computer app;\\n- a browser extension.\\n\\nIts main advantage is convenience: open the app, confirm the operation, and you are done. Convenience brings additional risk, however. The keys are on a device that regularly interacts with the internet and other programs. Be particularly cautious about wallets on your main work computer.\\n\\nYou download files, open documents, install programs and use a browser, Telegram and email. Running a malicious file once can install an infostealer, software designed to steal passwords, cookies, application data and other sensitive information. Holding significant funds on a computer you use every day is therefore unwise.\\n\\nThis does not mean a hot wallet is automatically unsafe. A separate smartphone or other device used exclusively for cryptocurrency, without other software installed, protects against the overwhelming majority of attacks.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n### Is a Cold Wallet Safe?\\n\\nBeginners are often told:\\n\\n>\\n\\nBuy a hardware wallet and everything will be safe.\\n\\nReality is more complicated. A cold wallet stores keys on a separate device and tries to keep them from being exposed to a computer or phone. This does protect against many common attacks. For example, a computer virus should not be able simply to read a private key from the hardware wallet.\\n\\nBut a cold wallet is neither a holy grail nor absolute protection. Vulnerabilities are periodically discovered in these devices’ software and firmware. Some affect secure generation of secret keys; others affect how transactions are displayed and confirmed.\\n\\nCounterfeit or pre-compromised devices also pose risks, as does the malicious software through which a user interacts with a wallet. Theft involving pre-compromised devices has increased many times over the last 2 years, with estimated losses in the hundreds of millions of dollars.\\n\\nA cold wallet therefore does not guarantee that your funds cannot be stolen. The central point is:\\n\\nno device purchase automatically makes your money invulnerable.\\n\\nSecurity is always a system of several protective layers.\\n\\n### A Hot Wallet Can Be Secure Enough\\n\\nA well-protected hot wallet on a separate device can be a reasonable option for an ordinary user, especially with a second protective layer: multisignature. This is where things become particularly useful.\\n\\n### What Is Multisignature?\\n\\nAn ordinary cryptocurrency wallet works roughly as follows:\\n\\n1 key → funds can be sent\\n\\nSteal that key and you control the funds. Multisignature, or multisig, requires several independent approvals. For example:\\n\\nDevice №1 + device №2 → transaction authorized\\n\\nThis is a 2/2 scheme:\\n\\nthere are two keys, and both signatures are needed to send money. Suppose one key is on one smartphone and the other is on a separate device. If an attacker infects the first device and gains access to its key, that is insufficient. The second signature is still required.\\n\\nStealing one phone is also insufficient. Compromising one wallet still does not let the attacker simply transfer the funds. To steal the money, the attacker must compromise two independent points simultaneously. Multisignature therefore substantially increases storage security.\\n\\n### The 2/2 Scheme Has a Drawback\\n\\nIf a transaction requires two signatures out of two:\\n\\n2/2\\n\\nand you lose access to one key, you can no longer sign a transaction. Protection against theft improves, but the risk of locking yourself out appears. Plan recovery for every key in advance. Other schemes also exist, such as:\\n\\n2/3\\n\\nThree independent keys exist, but any two are sufficient for a transaction. Losing one key does not block access to funds. Your choice depends on the amount, convenience and the risks you want to minimize.\\n\\nWho Can Reach Your Money?\\n\\nTap a key: stolen → lost → intact. You can transfer. The thief cannot transfer. Everything is intact. The thief must obtain two keys at once, a much harder task.\\n\\n### Which Should You Choose?\\n\\nA cold wallet isolates keys from an ordinary computer and protects against many kinds of malware. However, it introduces device-supplier and firmware-vulnerability risks that are difficult to control. A hot wallet is more convenient and, if configured properly, can also be sufficiently secure.\\n\\nMultisignature goes further, making one compromised device insufficient to steal the funds. Instead of asking:\\n\\n“Which wallet is safest?”\\n\\nask:\\n\\n“What would an attacker have to do to get my money?”\\n\\nIf the answer is:\\n\\n>\\n\\nsteal one password or access one computer,\\n\\nthe protection is weak. If the attacker must independently compromise multiple devices and keys, the task becomes much harder. That is the storage system to aim for. We will now examine it in practice:\\n\\nGuide 1 — creating and setting up an OKX wallet\\n\\nGuide 2 — creating a 2/2 multisignature wallet with transaction approval from two independent devices\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nWhere is cryptocurrency actually recorded?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Cold Wallet** — Personal crypto storage where only you hold the private key. This is how large amounts are stored, rather than on an exchange\\n- **Private Key** — What allows you to control the cryptocurrency at an address. The crypto itself is recorded on the blockchain; the wallet stores the keys, which are what you need to protect\\n- **Hot Wallet** — A wallet application on an internet-connected phone, computer or browser extension. Convenient, but its keys sit alongside everything you download; a separate device is considerably safer\\n- **Multisignature (multisig)** — A wallet requiring signatures from multiple devices for a transfer, for example 2 of 2 or 2 of 3. Stealing one key is still not enough to withdraw the money\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: Where is cryptocurrency actually recorded?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Inside the wallet application\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: On the blockchain; the wallet stores access for controlling it\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Inside a phone or Ledger\\n\\n**Question 2: Why is a cold wallet not an absolute guarantee of security?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Hardware wallets do not protect keys at all\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: User error, firmware, malware and device risks remain\\n- ⚪ Option 3: A cold wallet cannot be used without a bank\\n\\n**Question 3: What is the main advantage of a 2/2 multisignature wallet?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Stealing any single key is enough to transfer funds\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: An operation requires two independent approvals\\n- ⚪ Option 3: It completely eliminates the possibility of losing access to funds\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-03-1","prompt":"Where is cryptocurrency actually recorded?","options":[{"id":"base-03-1-1","label":"Inside the wallet application","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. On the blockchain; the wallet stores access for controlling it"},{"id":"base-03-1-2","label":"On the blockchain; the wallet stores access for controlling it","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. On the blockchain; the wallet stores access for controlling it"},{"id":"base-03-1-3","label":"Inside a phone or Ledger","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. On the blockchain; the wallet stores access for controlling it"}],"explanation":"On the blockchain; the wallet stores access for controlling it","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:603–606 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-03-2","prompt":"Why is a cold wallet not an absolute guarantee of security?","options":[{"id":"base-03-2-1","label":"Hardware wallets do not protect keys at all","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. User error, firmware, malware and device risks remain"},{"id":"base-03-2-2","label":"User error, firmware, malware and device risks remain","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. User error, firmware, malware and device risks remain"},{"id":"base-03-2-3","label":"A cold wallet cannot be used without a bank","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. User error, firmware, malware and device risks remain"}],"explanation":"User error, firmware, malware and device risks remain","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:608–611 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-03-3","prompt":"What is the main advantage of a 2/2 multisignature wallet?","options":[{"id":"base-03-3-1","label":"Stealing any single key is enough to transfer funds","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. An operation requires two independent approvals"},{"id":"base-03-3-2","label":"An operation requires two independent approvals","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. An operation requires two independent approvals"},{"id":"base-03-3-3","label":"It completely eliminates the possibility of losing access to funds","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. An operation requires two independent approvals"}],"explanation":"An operation requires two independent approvals","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:613–616 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":469,"lineEnd":621}}')}}]);